From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Clint Goudie-Nice <clint-goudie-nice@example.net>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 11:51:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] seastone: sensors.conf: limits for the SO-DIMM's JC-42.4
 thermal sensor

The DX010's SO-DIMM carries a JEDEC JC-42.4 temperature sensor on the
SMBus (jc42-i2c-1-18). Its limit registers come up at 0 C, so with the
DIMM at its normal ~35 C libsensors reports HIGH and CRIT, and pmon's
sensord logs it about once a minute:

  ALERT pmon#sensord: Sensor alarm: Chip jc42-i2c-1-18: temp1: 35.8 C
  (min = 0.0 C, max = 0.0 C) [ALARM]

-- about 1,400 syslog lines a day, on the stock image and every build
since. This gives the chip the limits of JEDEC's DRAM temperature range,
85 C (normal) and 95 C (extended), and a label. The registers take the
write (no event lock set), and lm-sensors.sh applies the platform file
with `sensors -s` at every pmon start, so no other change is needed.

Measured on a DX010 (202511, applied by hand through pmon's lm-sensors
program): temp1_max 85000, temp1_crit 95000, every alarm bit 0, and no
sensord alarm line in syslog over the following 11 minutes (before: one
a minute); `show platform temperature` unchanged.
---
 device/celestica/x86_64-cel_seastone-r0/sensors.conf | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/device/celestica/x86_64-cel_seastone-r0/sensors.conf b/device/celestica/x86_64-cel_seastone-r0/sensors.conf
index d92d6534e..f8e14cac6 100644
--- a/device/celestica/x86_64-cel_seastone-r0/sensors.conf
+++ b/device/celestica/x86_64-cel_seastone-r0/sensors.conf
@@ -71,3 +71,12 @@ chip "emc2305-i2c-*-4d"
     label fan5 "fan3"
     label fan3 "fan4"
     label fan1 "fan5"
+
+# The SO-DIMM's SPD thermal sensor (JEDEC JC-42.4 TSE, i2c-1-0018). Its
+# limit registers come up at 0 C, so sensord reported its normal reading
+# as HIGH/CRIT about once a minute. JEDEC's normal DRAM range ends at 85 C
+# (extended to 95 C).
+chip "jc42-*"
+    label temp1 "SO-DIMM temp sensor"
+    set temp1_max 85
+    set temp1_crit 95
